                "text": "Let me understand the rule precisely from the verifier.\n\nColors: 8 = azure (called purple in code), 3 = green, 0 = black.\n\nFor each row:\n- find rightmost 8. target_start = 0 if none, else rightmost_8 + 1.\n- find green cells (3), min_c, max_c, length = max_c - min_c + 1.\n- if no green, skip.\n- clear original green positions (min_c..max_c) to 0.\n- place green block at target_start..target_start+length-1 (if within bounds).\n\nWait but careful: the green block placement could overwrite cells that were originally 8? Let's check: we set output[r][c]=3 for c in target_start..target_start+length-1. target_start = rightmost_8+1, so the cells to the right of rightmost 8 are... possibly 0. But if there are 8s to the right? No, rightmost 8 is the last 8. So after it could be 0s or greens. Hmm.\n\nAlso note: clearing happens before placing, and placement overwrites whatever.\n\nLet me verify example row 1: input [8,8,0,0,3]. rightmost 8 = col1. target_start=2. green cols=[4], length=1. clear col4 -> 0. place green at col2. output row [8,8,3,0,0]. Matches.\n\nRow 2: [0,8,8,0,3] -> rightmost 8 col2, target_start3, green length1, clear col4, place col3. output [0,8,8,3,0]. Matches.\n\nRow 0: no green, unchanged. Row 3: no green. Row 4: no green.\n\nGood. Note length is max_c-min_c+1, and it clears range min_c..max_c then places. If green block isn't contiguous, this still works with gap. But we should make contiguous inputs.\n\nNow I need to create 3 demonstration pairs. I can author inputs; environment computes outputs via verifier. I need to make them varied and demonstrate the rule well. Since Student sees only these 3 pairs, I should cover key cases:\n1. Row with no 8s and greens -> green placed at column 0.\n2. Row with 8s and green block of width >1 -> placed after rightmost 8.\n3. Some rows without green remain unchanged, mixed sizes.\n\nAlso need to be careful that placement fits within bounds (target_start+length <= cols). If not, code just truncates (only places if c<cols). Actually it clears min..max then places within cols. So it could lose cells. Avoid that.\n\nLet me design grids.\n\nLet me make grid sizes varied (3 pairs can be different shapes; rule says grid size preserved).\n\nPair 1: 5x5 like example but with width-2 green block.\nLet's craft:\nrow0: [8,0,8,3,3] -> rightmost 8 col2, target_start3, green min3 max4 length2. clear 3,4. place at 3,4 -> [8,0,8,3,3]. Hmm unchanged, not interesting. Let's move green elsewhere.\n\nLet's do:\nrow0: [8,0,8,0,3,3]? 6 cols.\nLet me use 5 cols.\n\nPair 1 (5x5):\nrow0: [8,0,8,0,0]  no green -> unchanged\nrow1: [8,0,0,3,3]  rightmost 8 col0, target_start1, green min3 max4 len2. clear 3,4, place at 1,2 -> [8,3,3,0,0]\nrow2: [0,8,0,3,3]  rightmost 8 col1, target1? target_start=2. green 3..4 -> clear, place at 2,3 -> [0,8,3,3,0]\nrow3: [0,0,0,0,0] unchanged\nrow4: [3,3,0,8,0]  rightmost 8 col3, target4, green min0 max1 len2. clear 0,1, place at 4,5 -> col4 only (since len2, one goes out). That's bad, truncation. Avoid.\n\nLet me reconsider row4: [3,3,0,8,8]? rightmost 8 col4, target_start5, len2 doesn't fit. Avoid green with 8 at right edge.\n\nLet's just make row with no 8: [3,3,0,0,0] -> target 0, green min0 max1 len2, clear 0,1, place at 0,1 -> unchanged. Hmm that shows no-8 case but output same as input, which is ambiguous. Better no-8 row with green not at left: [0,3,3,0,0] -> target 0, len2, clear 1,2, place 0,1 -> [3,3,0,0,0]. That demonstrates the no-8 rule.\n\nLet me finalize Pair 1 (5x5):\nrow0: [8,0,8,0,0] -> unchanged [8,0,8,0,0]\nrow1: [8,0,0,3,3] -> [8,3,3,0,0]\nrow2: [0,8,0,3,3] -> [0,8,3,3,0]\nrow3: [0,3,3,0,0] -> [3,3,0,0,0]  (no 8 case)\nrow4: [0,0,0,0,0] -> unchanged\n\nCheck lengths fit: row1 target=1 len2 -> 1,2 fits. row2 target=2 ->2,3 fits. row3 target=0 ->0,1 fits. Good.\n\nPair 2: different dimensions, 4x6.\nrow0: [8,8,0,0,0,0] unchanged\nrow1: [0,0,8,0,3,0] rightmost8 col2 target3 len1 clear4 place3 -> [0,0,8,3,0,0]\nrow2: [3,0,0,0,0,0] no 8, target0, len1, clear0 place0 -> unchanged. Hmm again identity. Let's make row2 green at different col: [0,0,0,3,0,0] no 8 -> target0, clear3, place0 -> [3,0,0,0,0,0]. Good.\nrow3: [8,0,0,0,3,3] rightmost8 col0 target1 len2 clear4,5 place1,2 -> [8,3,3,0,0,0].\n\nCheck fits. Good.\n\nActually wait: clear only happens within min_c..max_c. In row3 clear 4,5. place 1,2. Good. But note original col0=8 remains.\n\nPair 3: maybe 6x4 (tall).\nrow0: [0,8,0,0] unchanged (no green)\nrow1: [0,8,0,3] rightmost8 col1 target2 len1 clear3 place2 -> [0,8,3,0]\nrow2: [8,0,3,3] rightmost8 col0 target1 len2 clear2,3 place1,2 -> [8,3,3,0]\nrow3: [0,0,0,3] no 8 target0 len1 clear3 place0 -> [3,0,0,0]\nrow4: [8,8,8,0] no green unchanged\nrow5: [0,0,3,3] no 8 target0 len2 clear2,3 place0,1 -> [3,3,0,0]\n\nAll fit.\n\nThese three pairs give variety: width-1 and width-2 green blocks, no-8 rows, rows without green, different grid sizes. Good teaching.\n\nLet me double check the verifier handles the \"rightmost 8\" computed on original grid, not output. Yes, uses grid[r][c]. Good.\n\nOne subtlety: if placement overlaps original green positions that were cleared\u2014fine.\n\nAnother subtlety: what if rightmost 8 is to the right of green? Then target_start could be > min_c. E.g., [3,8,0,...]. rightmost8 col1, target2, green min0 max0 len1, clear0, place2. Fine.\n\nI'll submit these three inputs.\n\nLet me write them.\n\nPair 1:\n[[8,0,8,0,0],\n [8,0,0,3,3],\n [0,8,0,3,3],\n [0,3,3,0,0],\n [0,0,0,0,0]]\n\nPair 2:\n[[8,8,0,0,0,0],\n [0,0,8,0,3,0],\n [0,0,0,3,0,0],\n [8,0,0,0,3,3]]\n\nWait row2 [0,0,0,3,0,0]: green min3 max3 len1, clear3, place at target0 -> [3,0,0,0,0,0]. Good.\n\nPair 3:\n[[0,8,0,0],\n [0,8,0,3],\n [8,0,3,3],\n [0,0,0,3],\n [8,8,8,0],\n [0,0,3,3]]\n\nLet me call the tool three times. They're independent, so I can call all in one block.\n\nThen I need to call start_exam? The instructions say \"call start_exam when ready.\" But there's no start_exam in the tools list p